      "desc": "In 2026, the standard narrative was clear: RX 580 cannot run AI. Analysis of locked ecosystems and Vulkan acceleration viability.",
      "html": "<p style=\"font-size:.88rem;color:#94a3b8;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1.25rem\">In 2026, the standard narrative was clear: <strong style=\"color:#fff\">RX 580 cannot run AI</strong>. The available ecosystems were:</p>\n  <div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Solution</th><th>Status for RX 580</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>CUDA (Nvidia)</td><td>❌ Nvidia Exclusive</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>ROCm (AMD official)</td><td>❌ Removed support for Polaris/GCN4 in version 5.x</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>DirectML (Microsoft)</td><td>❌ Abandoned before maturing — unstable, slow</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>OpenVINO (Intel)</td><td>❌ Incompatible with Forge on Windows</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n  <div class=\"tbl\" style=\"margin-top:.9rem\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Status (before the solution)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>LLM</td><td>Pure CPU — 25 to 40 minutes per response</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Image generation</td><td>ComfyUI via CPU — 50+ minutes per image on HDD</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>RX 580</td><td>Idle, 0% AI usage</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Slow HDD (critical I/O bottleneck)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n  <div class=\"card acc\" style=\"margin-top:1.25rem\">\n    <blockquote style=\"font-size:.88rem;color:#94a3b8;font-style:italic;line-height:1.9;border-left:3px solid var(--r);padding-left:.9rem\">\"Polaris is obsolete. Only good for mining. Old AMD doesn't run AI. Swap the card.\"</blockquote>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:.9rem;font-size:.88rem;color:#e2e8f0;font-weight:600\">This belief was false. The computational power was always there. The problem was the software, not the hardware.</p>\n  </div>\n  <p style=\"margin-top:1.25rem;font-size:.88rem;color:#94a3b8;line-height:1.9\">The <code style=\"color:var(--r)\">ggml</code> project (base of <code style=\"color:var(--r)\">llama.cpp</code> and <code style=\"color:var(--r)\">stable-diffusion.cpp</code>) uses Vulkan as a GPU backend — an open standard that works on any modern GPU. The RX 580 has supported Vulkan 1.x since its original 2017 drivers. ROCm and CUDA are unnecessary.</p>\n  <p style=\"margin-top:.9rem;font-size:.88rem;color:#94a3b8;line-height:1.9\">For larger models (FLUX.1 Schnell 16GB, for example), the solution is to run via <strong style=\"color:#fff\">Xeon CPU + abundant ECC RAM</strong> through ComfyUI on WSL2, with a symbolic link to the models on Windows — without duplicating files, without wasting space.</p>"
